@*
    gcroot retention paths (5.3, DATA_CONTRACT.md §4.3) -- the chains keeping one object alive,
    merged into a trie so shared ancestry is drawn once and forks where the paths diverge.

    THE ONE RULE THIS TEMPLATE EXISTS TO ENFORCE (docs/GCROOT_TRUNCATION.md):

      An empty result is not evidence. A search that exhausted its node budget and a search that
      covered everything reachable and found nothing produce the same empty list, and the original
      defect was a formatter that could not tell them apart and guessed "unrooted, eligible for
      collection" for both -- the most misleading answer this tool can give, on precisely the large
      leak dumps it exists for.

      So this view never asks whether the tree is empty. It switches on GCRootTree.Outcome, which
      GCRootTreeBuilder computes once from (paths, truncated) together. The words "unrooted" and
      "eligible for collection" appear in exactly one arm of that switch -- Unrooted, which means
      Truncated == false AND no paths -- and nowhere else in this file.

    Fully nested up front: one HeapAnalyzer.GetGCRoots call returns the whole tree, so _GcRootNodes
    recurses into real <ul>/<li>/<details> rather than going through the lazy _TreeNodes.cshtml
    machinery. Same "no style=" discipline as every other fragment.
*@
@using DotNetDump.Core.Trees
@model DotNetDump.Web.Rendering.GCRootTreeModel

<div id="v-gcroot">
    @switch (Model.Tree.Outcome)
    {
        case GCRootOutcome.Inconclusive:
            <div class="dn-tree-banner" role="alert">
                <span>
                    <strong>Inconclusive — the search did not finish.</strong>
                    It gave up after visiting @Display.Count(Model.Tree.NodesVisited) objects, having exhausted its
                    traversal budget. No retention path was found, but that is not a finding: the object may well be
                    held by something the search never reached. Re-run with an unlimited budget for an answer that
                    means something (it holds every visited node in memory, roughly 40 bytes each, so it is not free).
                </span>
                <a class="dn-btn" href="@Model.ConclusiveHref">Re-run unlimited</a>
            </div>
            break;

        case GCRootOutcome.RootedPartial:
            <div class="dn-tree-banner" role="alert">
                <span>
                    <strong>Partial — the search did not finish.</strong>
                    The @(Model.Tree.PathCount == 1 ? "path" : "paths") below @(Model.Tree.PathCount == 1 ? "is" : "are")
                    real, but after visiting @Display.Count(Model.Tree.NodesVisited) objects the search exhausted its
                    budget while looking for further independent paths. There may be more references holding this
                    object than are shown here.
                </span>
                <a class="dn-btn" href="@Model.ConclusiveHref">Re-run unlimited</a>
            </div>
            break;

        case GCRootOutcome.Unrooted:
            @* The only arm licensed to draw this conclusion: the search completed, so absence of a
               path really is absence of a path. *@
            <div class="dn-empty">
                <div class="dn-empty__title">No retention path to @Display.Address(Model.Tree.TargetAddress).</div>
                <div>
                    The search completed after visiting @Display.Count(Model.Tree.NodesVisited) objects and found no GC
                    root that reaches this one, so it is unrooted and eligible for collection — or it was collected
                    already and the address is stale.
                </div>
            </div>
            break;
    }

    @if (Model.Tree.Roots.Count > 0)
    {
        <ul class="dn-tree" role="group" aria-label="Retention paths">
            <partial name="/Views/Fragments/_GcRootNodes.cshtml" model="Model.Tree.Roots" />
        </ul>

        <div class="dn-note">
            @Display.Count(Model.Tree.PathCount) retention @(Model.Tree.PathCount == 1 ? "path" : "paths")
            to @Display.Address(Model.Tree.TargetAddress),
            read root first. @Display.Count(Model.Tree.NodesVisited) objects visited.
        </div>
    }
</div>
